nodecode-hass-statistics is a rewrite of goncami's goncami/hermes-homeassistant-statistics:

  https://github.com/goncami/hermes-homeassistant-statistics
  commit c6a64211c5411eeb4a15d912f9640906bc7c6ee3
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 Nous Research (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's two model-facing tools — ha_list_statistics and
ha_get_statistics — with their argument schemas and answer shapes, the two
Recorder commands they run over the instance's WebSocket API
(recorder/list_statistic_ids and recorder/statistics_during_period), the input
validation (statistic ids, ISO-8601 timestamps that require a timezone, the
period and value vocabularies, distinct ids and types preserved in order), the
operator allow/deny lists with their exact, glob and family-prefix matching and
the denylist's precedence, the three budgets (ids per query, rows estimated
before and counted after, and the serialized answer's bytes), the
redirect-free and cookie-free exchange with a bounded close, and the fixed
error categories that never reflect the instance's own text. The Python
(aiohttp, asyncio) is Common Lisp here: websocket-driver for the one
authenticated exchange, one section declaration for the settings, and two
nlk:define-contract declarations for the tools' arguments, so the schema the
model is shown and the body that runs are one shape.

Left out: the plugin's configuration surface, which read the values from the
harness's environment variables; a cell has a config section instead, so the
URL, the token's variable or file, the two lists and the three budgets are
named there, and a token is never written inline. Also left out are the
plugin's own 91-test suite, its VALIDATION.json and its manifest checks, which
belong to the Hermes plugin format rather than to the tools; the cell carries
its own tests against a stubbed socket. The two tools' behaviour, and the data
they reach, are the same.
